"Surah Qaf, like Surah Sad, begins with a letter and an oath by the Quran. These letters are allegorical (mutashabih); their meaning is known only to Allah. According to Fakhr al-Din al-Razi, these letters serve as an alert to draw the listener's attention, and just as the rationale behind certain acts of worship cannot be grasped by human intellect, reciting these letters is an act of submitting to the divine command purely with the intention of worship. Allah swearing by these letters confers a distinct honor upon them.\n\nAl-Razi lists five points regarding oaths sworn by letters: that oaths are taken either by things or by letters; that in oaths sworn by letters the oath particle is not mentioned; that the oaths are sworn by simple letters rather than by composite entities of things; that oaths sworn by letters occur in 28 surahs, whereas oaths sworn by things occur in 14 surahs; and that while oaths sworn by letters appear throughout, oaths sworn by things generally occur in the latter part.\n\nThe narration stating that the letter Qaf is the name of a mountain is considered weak. Al-Razi and al-Alusi oppose this view; according to al-Razi, the existence of this mountain has not been empirically observed, and al-Alusi shares the same opinion. Since the narration regarding Mount Qaf does not trace back to the Prophet (peace be upon him), it is more appropriate to consider the letter allegorical (mutashabih) and regard it as a symbol of Allah's omnipotence. However, according to a narration from Ibn Abbas, Qaf is a mountain encircling the earth and can be interpreted as the atmosphere.\n\nTurning to the exegesis of the verses, it is stated that Allah swore by the glorious Qaf (Qaf al-Majid), that the disbelievers were astonished that a warner had come to them from among themselves, and that they deemed resurrection after death far-fetched. It is expressed that Allah's knowledge encompasses what the earth diminishes of man and that everything is preserved in the Preserved Tablet (al-Lawh al-Mahfuz). This knowledge demonstrates that resurrection is not impossible.\n\nAllah commands looking at the sky and the earth; it is recounted that the sky is without rifts, that the earth is spread out and stabilized with mountains, and that every beautiful pair of plants is brought forth. All of these serve as a lesson and a reminder for every servant who turns to their Lord. The sending down of blessed water from the sky to revive dead land is an analogue to the emergence of the dead from their graves.\n\nIt is recalled that earlier nations—Noah, al-Rass, Thamud, 'Ad, Pharaoh, Lot, and Tubba'—suffered punishment as a result of denying the prophets. It is emphasized that there was no incapacity in the initial creation, and thus re-creation is entirely possible. The idea of the continuous renewal of creation at every moment influenced thinkers such as Muhyiddin Ibn Arabi and Descartes.\n\nBecause Allah created man, He knows the whisperings of his soul; He is closer to him than his jugular vein. Two angels on the right and left record every word. When the stupor of death arrives, it is said, \"This is what you used to evade.\" The Trumpet (Sur) is blown, and every soul arrives with a driver and a witness. On that day, the veil of heedlessness is lifted, and vision becomes sharp. The companion angel says, \"This is ready.\" Allah commands that obstinate ingrates be cast into Hell; Satan pleads that he did not cause him to transgress. Allah declares that disputing in His presence is of no avail.\n\nAllah knows everything that a person's soul whispers to him. Whispering (waswasah) encompasses hidden emotions, memories, and misgivings passing through a person's inner self. Allah knows even these most concealed thoughts, for He is the Creator. He is closer to man than his jugular vein. This closeness is in terms of knowledge and power; it is not a spatial proximity. Scholars have interpreted this expression as a proximity of knowledge or power. Al-Raghib states that closeness is used in the sense of power. Ibn Jarir reports that Arabic linguists disagreed over the meaning of this verse.\n\nThere are differing views regarding the expression habl al-warid. Warid is the vein in the neck, while habl means rope or cord. In anatomy, al-warid is the vein that carries dark blood to the heart, whereas al-watin is the aorta. This vein swells in moments of anger. Al-Zamakhshari states that habl al-warid can also be understood as the base of the neck. Al-Raghib, on the other hand, notes that what is meant by habl al-warid is the soul (ruh). The arrival of dark blood to the heart pertains to purification and a new creation, pointing toward the states of the Hereafter.\n\nThe views of Muhyiddin Ibn Arabi regarding Allah's closeness are relayed. According to him, the presence of the Closest One is with His essence for the people of mystical unveiling (kashf). Yet this closeness entails distance for the sinner. Allah is closer to us than we are to ourselves, for union is only through Him. The servant draws near to Allah through supererogatory devotions (nawafil) and becomes His hearing and His sight. However, this does not mean that the servant becomes Allah; there remains an essential distinction between them. In the concept of the Unity of Existence (Wahdat al-Wujud), the Creator and the creation are not identical; everything belongs to Allah, but being Allah is something entirely different.\n\nIn the continuation of the verse, mention is made of two angels who record every word of man. They sit on the right and on the left, keeping record. Every word, whether good or evil, is recorded by an observer beside him. However, the whisperings within the soul—inner speech that has not reached the level of intent and decision—are not written down. On this matter, Imam Malik stated that everything is written down, whereas some scholars noted that only matters entailing accountability are recorded. The style of the verse depicts man like an interrogated criminal or a patient on the verge of death.\n\nThereafter, death and the Hereafter are depicted. When the stupor of death comes in truth, Allah is closer than the jugular vein. The Trumpet is blown, and this is the Day of Retribution. Every soul arrives accompanied by a driver and a witness angel. On that day, it is said to the disbeliever: 'You were in heedlessness of this.' Satan says, 'I did not lead him astray; rather, he was in his own deep misguidance.' Allah commands: do not dispute in My presence; the word is not altered, and He is not unjust to His servants.\n\nFinally, Hell and Paradise are depicted. Allah says to Hell, 'Are you full?' and it asks, 'Are there any more?' Paradise, on the other hand, is brought near to the righteous. It is for those who turn to Allah, fear the Most Merciful (al-Rahman) unseen, and come with a penitent heart. It is said to them, 'Enter it in peace; this is the Day of Eternity.' There they will have whatever they desire, and with Us is yet more.\n\nAs described in verse 30, Hell will be questioned on that day as to whether it has been filled, and it will ask, 'Are there any more?' This marks the fulfillment of Allah's promise: 'Surely I will fill Hell entirely.' Hell will be filled with those cast into it in groups from among the jinn and mankind, yet its momentum will not slacken; out of fury and severity toward sinners, it will crave even more. This question and answer depict the terror of Hell. While some scholars regard this as literal reality, others, such as al-Alusi, share this view. In a hadith narrated from Anas in Bukhari and Muslim, it is recounted that Hell repeatedly asks, 'Are there any more?' until its Mighty Lord places His foot, whereupon it contracts and cries, 'Enough, enough!' In Paradise, there remains continual surplus space, and Allah creates a new creation to dwell there.\n\nIn verses 31–33, it is stated that Paradise is brought near for the righteous (al-muttaqun) and is not far off. This refutes the claim of the disbelievers who said, 'This is a far-off return.' The righteous are those who come with a heart turned to Allah, who fear the Most Merciful unseen, and who preserve His limits. 'Awwab' means one who turns repeatedly in repentance. These verses emphasize that Paradise promised to the righteous is near at hand.\n\nIn verses 34–35, it is conveyed that the righteous will be told, 'Enter in peace and security,' and that they will abide therein eternally. That day is the Day of Eternity; it has no end. In Paradise, they will have whatever they wish, and with Allah there is yet more; this surplus consists of blessings no eye has seen, no ear has heard, and no human heart has ever conceived. This means that the inhabitants of Paradise will be granted more than they desire, or that they will be lavishly honored in the presence of Allah.\n\nVerses 36-38 remind that earlier generations who were mightier than them were destroyed. They had traversed throughout the lands, seeking a place of escape from death. In what is recounted in this surah, there is indeed a lesson for anyone who possesses a heart or listens attentively. It is stated that Allah created the heavens, the earth, and all that is between them in six days and was not touched by weariness; this refutes the claims of the Jews.\n\nIn verses 39-40, the Prophet Muhammad (peace be upon him) is commanded to be patient and to glorify his Lord with praise by performing prayer before the rising of the sun (the morning prayer), before its setting (the noon and afternoon prayers), and during the night (the evening and night prayers). Furthermore, glorifying Him after the prayers is recommended. This points to the appointed times of prayer and to glorification (tasbih).\n\nIn verses 41-44, the day when a caller will call out from a nearby place is described. This caller is Israfil or Gabriel (Jibril). On that day, everyone will hear this call and emerge from their graves. The earth will split open, and people will rush forth swiftly. This gathering is easy for Allah. According to al-Baydawi, the Day of Emerging is one of the names of the Day of Resurrection. The splitting of the earth is open to different interpretations: it cracks open either from beneath them or from above them. This gathering is tremendous, yet it is easy for Allah.\n\nIn verse 45, the Prophet Muhammad (peace be upon him) is commanded not to act as a compeller over what the disbelievers say, but rather to remind with the Quran those who fear Allah's warning. Thus, they may strive to protect themselves for that Day, come with a heart turned toward Allah, and enter the Abode of Eternity in peace."